<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basti (Iberia)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basti was the chief town of the Bastetani, an Iberian people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the hill of Cerro Cepero near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. It continued as a Roman town. Its necropolis yielded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-baza">Lady of Baza</a>, a painted stone statue of an enthroned woman used as a cinerary urn, found in 1971.</p></div>
